================================================================================
ANALYSIS OF THE LAPTOP OUTPUTS (highascg4790)
================================================================================

MY MAIN THEORY WAS WRONG. I said the box was CPU-starved and the Node event loop
was blocked, based on the ~450ms spacing between commands in the log you pasted.
Your measurements refute that:

  event-loop lag  2.8 ms   (healthy here: 0.2 ms - same order, fine)
  AMCP round-trip 1 0 0 0 1 ms   (healthy here: 1 0 0 0 2 ms - identical)

The control path is NOT slow. Caspar answers instantly and the event loop is not
blocked. So whatever the 450ms spacing in that startup log was, it was not
starvation, and I no longer have a confirmed explanation for it - the most likely
candidate is the CG orphan sweep serialisation I fixed in b7a6c99, which was
still present when you captured that log.

WHAT THE NUMBERS DO SHOW

  casparcg alone: 481-521% CPU = FIVE of your twelve cores, 866 MB RSS
  load1 11.31 on 12 cores - load is essentially equal to core count
  but %Cpu(s) shows 41.2% idle, so it is contention, not a hard ceiling
  PSI cpu: some avg10=28.55, full=0.00 -> tasks wait ~28% of the time, nothing
           fully stalls
  PSI io and memory: full=0.00, total barely moves -> NOT swapping, NOT disk-bound
  swap: 0 B used of 8 G. memory: 8.2 Gi free. Memory is not the problem.
  governor: already "performance", 3.84 GHz of a 4.60 GHz max. Not a power
           management problem - nothing to fix there.
  renderer: Mesa 25.2.8 AMD - real hardware GL, NOT llvmpipe. Good news; software
           rendering would have been far worse.
  dropped/late frames: none logged. Caspar is keeping its frame clock.

  thermal_zone0 (acpitz): 86000 = 86 C. That is hot. acpitz is usually not the
  CPU die sensor so treat it as indicative, but a laptop sitting at 86 C under
  sustained load has no thermal headroom left.

SO WHAT IS ACTUALLY LAGGING
The control path measures clean, so the lag you feel is in the VIDEO path, not
in highascg talking to Caspar. Caspar is holding 5 cores to run two 1080p50
channels with gpu_texture:false ("frame copied to host for rendering") on
integrated AMD graphics - a full CPU readback of a 1920x1080 frame 50 times a
second, twice over. That is the load, and it is the thing to attack.

RANKED, BY EXPECTED EFFECT
  1. Drop to 1080p2500, or to a single channel. This is the 5-core item. Cheapest
     test available and it needs no code change.
  2. 86 C with no headroom - check fans/vents. A thermally limited laptop will
     get progressively worse the longer a show runs.
  3. Two ffmpeg processes at 28% and 26.9% (children of highascg, pid 17167) are
     the preview JPEG encoders, roughly half a core between them. Lowering the
     preview fps would buy that back.
  4. FIXED already: nvidia-smi was running repeatedly on this AMD box. The GPU
     probe cached only SUCCESSFUL results, so with no NVIDIA card present every
     single host-stats poll spawned another doomed nvidia-smi. That is why
     gpu.source is null in your output while nvidia-smi shows in the process
     list. Now caches the negative too.
  5. Minor: 2 zombie processes, and the media disk is only 7.98 GB total
     (7.64 GB free) - that will fill quickly once you record anything.
